<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/docs/Snippet.abide'
import Console from './Console.abide'
import consoleSource from './Console.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The four levels and the default namespace">
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`log('sending')
log.debug('cache table', { entries: cache.size })
log.info('invoice paid', { id: '4310' })
log.warning('retrying', { attempt: 2 })
log.error('mailer refused', { error })`} />

        <p><code>log</code> on its own is a message under the default namespace, which is
        <code>APP_NAME</code>. The four named forms are the levels, and every one of them takes
        whatever arguments you give it — a message and a record of structured data is the shape that
        reads well in both output formats.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A namespace is turned on one at a time">
        <Example
            id="log-channels"
            title="`log.named` and `DEBUG` — one namespace on, one off"
            summary="A CRM's own log. Send an invoice, then press Poll — only one of them has a namespace that is on."
            files={{
                'Console.abide': consoleSource,
            }}
        >
            <Console />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 gate, 0 filters</em> — and the gate is at the call, so a line nothing selected
        never builds its argument object.</p>

        <p><code>log.named(name)</code> hands back a <code>Logger</code> — the same shape as
        <code>log</code>, bound to a namespace. <code>DEBUG</code> decides which namespaces emit,
        with the comma-and-wildcard grammar the ecosystem already uses, so
        <code>DEBUG=dogfood:billing,abide:*</code> is a sentence anybody who has used the convention
        can already read. A namespace starts with the app's name — this app is <code>dogfood</code> —
        on the server and in the browser alike.</p>

        <p><code>log.enabled()</code> asks whether a gated line under this namespace would be
        written. It is for the case where <strong>building the argument is the expensive
        part</strong>:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`if (log.enabled()) log.debug('table', { rows: expensiveSnapshot() })`} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="abide's own namespaces">
        <p>Every one is namespaced <code>abide:</code>, so <code>DEBUG=abide:*</code> turns on the
        framework and nothing of yours, and <code>DEBUG=abide:request</code> turns on one lane.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Levels</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>abide:request</code></td><td><code>debug</code></td><td>one line per request</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:socket</code></td><td><code>debug</code></td><td>one line per frame on the socket mux</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:lifecycle</code></td><td><code>debug</code> <code>error</code></td><td>a lifecycle that ran</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:principal</code></td><td><code>debug</code> <code>warning</code></td><td>a principal set or cleared</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:health</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>an <code>onHealth</code> that threw or answered a non-object</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:render</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>an <code>error.abide</code> that itself failed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:refuse</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>a message formatter that threw</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:reactive</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>a failed revalidation over a value still being served</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:watch</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>a <code>watch</code> effect or <code>Disposer</code> that threw</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:hydrate</code> / <code>abide:navigate</code></td><td><code>warning</code></td><td>the browser lane</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The <code>warning</code> namespaces are worth reading as a list, because each is a
        failure that is <strong>otherwise silent</strong>: a stale value still being served, an
        effect that threw after its first run, an error page that could not render. None of them
        stops the app, and none of them shows up anywhere else.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`log.records` is the lines kept">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type LogRecord = {
    time: string
    level: 'debug' | 'info' | 'warning' | 'error'
    namespace: string
    message: string
    trace: string
}`} />

        <p><code>log.records</code> is an ordinary <code>reactive</code> holding the lines kept as
        an array, each line appended as it is written (28.1) — which is why an admin page showing
        the last hundred lines <strong>subscribes</strong> rather than polls. There is no second
        mechanism for reading logs back.</p>

        <p>In a template it is a member access rather than a name, so the read is spelled out:</p>

        <Snippet source={consoleSource} path="Console.abide" from={['{#for record of']} />

        <p><code>ABIDE_MAX_LOG_BUFFER_COUNT</code> is how many of the latest lines it keeps, the
        oldest cut away in one move rather than shifted off per line, and they live in memory —
        bounded, and gone when the process is. Anything that has to outlive the process goes to a
        collector, which is what the machine format is for.</p>

        <p>abide serves none of it over http. An app that wants its log read from outside declares a
        <code>GET</code> over <code>log.records</code> like any other, behind whatever rung decides
        who may read it, and a reader of that <code>GET</code> is followed as any value is (18.20) —
        <code>abide watch</code> included.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two formats, chosen for the reader">
        <p><code>ABIDE_LOG_FORMAT</code> picks <code>tsv</code> or <code>json</code> for a
        collector; unset, the output is the readable form for a terminal. <code>NO_COLOR</code>
        refuses ansi anywhere and <code>FORCE_COLOR</code> asks for the readable form off a pipe —
        the two conventions a terminal tool is expected to honor, and honoring them is cheaper
        than explaining why it does not.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/configuration')}>Configuration</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/app/follow-a-request-across-services')}>Tracing</a> — the id every
            one of these carries</li>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/watch-a-room-from-the-terminal')}>Watch a channel</a> —
            following a <code>GET</code> from a terminal</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/helpers')}>Helpers</a> — <code>log</code> beside
            <code>csp</code>, <code>url</code> and <code>navigate</code></li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
